\description{
Estimate CATE scores for binary, count and survival outcomes. Binary scores
include arm-specific logistic regression, doubly robust two-regression estimation and
boosting. Count and survival methods include both outcome-regression and
doubly robust scores. Continuous outcomes are not yet implemented.
}
\details{
The table summarizes the available score-fitting methods. "Yes" in the
final column indicates a doubly robust score estimator, subject to the
assumptions described below; "--" means that the method is not available
for that outcome.

\tabular{lllll}{
\strong{Score method} \tab \strong{Binary} \tab \strong{Count} \tab \strong{Survival} \tab \strong{Doubly robust} \cr
\code{twoReg} \tab Yes \tab Yes \tab Yes \tab Yes \cr
\code{contrastReg} \tab -- \tab Yes \tab Yes \tab Yes \cr
\code{logistic} \tab Yes \tab -- \tab -- \tab No \cr
\code{poisson} \tab -- \tab Yes \tab Yes \tab No \cr
\code{negBin} \tab -- \tab Yes \tab -- \tab No \cr
\code{boosting} \tab Yes \tab Yes \tab Yes \tab No \cr
\code{randomForest} \tab -- \tab -- \tab Yes \tab No \cr
\code{coxph}, \code{weibull}, \code{log_normal} \tab -- \tab -- \tab Yes \tab No \cr
}

Two-regression and contrast regression share cross-fitted treatment-model
predictions. When their outcome methods match, they also share the
cross-fitted outcome predictions; otherwise, each outcome method is fitted
separately on the same folds. A T-learner uses outcome models fitted on the
full fitting sample, so it does not reuse the cross-fitted models. When two
distinct initial outcome methods are used, \code{outcome_methods} records
the method for each score. For stochastic outcome methods, random-number
consumption can differ from fitting each score in a separate call.

The doubly robust methods combine outcome and treatment models. Their
robustness to misspecification of one of these models is conditional on
identification, positivity, and method-specific model and regularity
assumptions. For example, binary \code{twoReg} estimates a linear projection
of the conditional risk difference; this projection equals the conditional
risk difference only if the latter is linear in the specified design.
Truncating propensity scores can affect the robustness property. Survival
methods also require appropriate censoring assumptions and a correctly
specified censoring model.

This table describes \emph{score fitting}. Subgroup effects can be estimated
using doubly robust methods even when the score used to define the subgroups
is not doubly robust. Neither score fitting nor subgroup estimates calculated
on the fitting data constitute independent validation; see \code{\link{catecv}}.

For binary response, see the Binary outcomes section below and \code{\link{catefitbinary}()}.
For count response, see details in \code{\link{catefitcount}()}.
For survival response, see details in \code{\link{catefitsurv}()}.
}
\section{Binary outcomes}{

For binary outcomes, the default logistic T-learner
fits a separate logistic outcome model in each treatment arm and subtracts
their predicted risks. \code{"twoReg"} cross-fits
separate logistic outcome models and a treatment model, then projects the
augmented inverse-propensity outcomes for each arm onto the covariates in
\code{benefit_model} (or \code{outcome_model} by default) using linear regression. The score is the difference of
these projections, averaged over \code{B} repetitions of \code{Kfold} folds.
It estimates the linear projection of the conditional risk difference if
either the treatment model or both outcome models are correct, under
identification and positivity assumptions. Propensity score truncation can
affect this property. The projection need not lie between -1 and 1 and
equals the conditional risk difference only if that difference is linear in
the specified design. The difference between arm coefficients is returned in
\code{coefficients$twoReg} and by \code{coef()}; the separate arm coefficients
are stored in \code{fit$twoReg$coefficients}. Logistic model coefficients
for each arm are on the log-odds scale and stored in \code{fit$logistic}; they are not
linear risk-difference score coefficients. Boosting does not have a linear
score coefficient vector. With neither \code{twoReg} nor another linear
binary score, \code{coef()} returns \code{NULL}.
\code{"boosting"} fits
separate Bernoulli gradient-boosting models in each treatment arm, with
the number of trees selected by five-fold cross-validation. All three scores
are predicted risk differences; subgroup effects are doubly robust risk
differences. Binary boosting requires at least one outcome-model covariate
and both outcome values in each arm. Its fitted models and selected tree
counts are stored in \code{fit$boosting}. Scores and subgroup effects are
estimated on the same data; they are not independent validation. For extra boosting controls,
see \code{\link{boosting-control}}.
Subgroup fits without both arms or with rank-deficient models return NA.
}

\examples{
# Count outcome
fit_1 <- catefit(response = "count",
                 data = countExample,
                 outcome_model = y ~ age + female + previous_treatment +
                                  previous_cost + previous_number_relapses +
                                  offset(log(years)),
                 outcome_method = "poisson",
                 benefit_method = "t_learner",
                 ps_model = trt ~ age + previous_treatment,
                 higher.y = TRUE,
                 seed = 999)

coef(fit_1)

\donttest{
# Survival outcome
library(survival)
tau0 <- with(survivalExample,
                 min(quantile(y[trt == "drug1"], 0.95), quantile(y[trt == "drug0"], 0.95)))

fit_2 <- catefit(response = "survival",
                 data = survivalExample,
                 outcome_model = Surv(y, d) ~ age + female + previous_cost +
                                           previous_number_relapses,
                 outcome_method = c("poisson", "boosting", "random_forest"),
                 benefit_method = rep("t_learner", 3),
                 ps_model = trt ~ age + previous_treatment,
                 censoring_model = ~ age + previous_cost + previous_treatment,
                 tau0 = tau0, higher.y = TRUE, seed = 999, n.cores = 1)

coef(fit_2)

}
}
